From SiegelEricR at uams.edu  Fri Nov  1 19:44:20 2002
From: SiegelEricR at uams.edu (Siegel, Eric R)
Date: Fri, 01 Nov 2002 13:44:20 -0600
Subject: microarray power and biological variability
Message-ID: <4E4DCE569DB2474CA8F145530C985448DCE960@exchange1.ad.uams.edu>

To Phillip Staford,

I was forwarded your e-mail to the S-news group about running the pilot study 
on 5 mice using 15 microarrays.  You asked the question whether the [squared] 
standard deviation across all 15 microarrays would yield the biological 
variance?  The answer is, No, it would yield the total variance, and you would 
have to break the total variance into Variance Components using a 
random-effects or mixed-effects model.  One variance component would be the 
biological variation; the other would be the technical variation.  I know how 
to do this in SAS, but not in S-Plus.  However, the S-plus procedures for 
estimating Variance Components should be covered in Pinheiro and Bates' book, 
"Mixed Effects in S and S-Plus, 1st ed.", 2000, Springer-Verlag, NY.
In more general terms, I think your plan for 5 mice and 3 microarrays per mouse 
is a good one.  To get a better estimate on biological variation, I would 
recommend just 2 microarrays per mouse, and as many mice as possible under that 
constraint.  If the maximum number of chips you can use is 15, then that's 7 
mice x 2 chips/mouse.  This does, of course, sacrifice precision of estimate 
for the technical variation relative to your plan for 5 mice x 3 chips/mouse, 
but it still provides an estimate (and the precision of estimate shouldn't be 
degraded all that much).  Don't recommend one chip per mouse, because then your 
variance will still be total variance, and you won't have any degrees of 
freedom for estimating (and subtracting off) the component due to technical 
variation.
Sincerely,
Eric Siegel, UAMS Division of Biometry.    

