From: naomi at stat.psu.edu (Naomi Altman)
Date: Fri, 01 Nov 2002 16:52:45 -0500
Subject: Re: microarray power and biological variability
In-Reply-To: <4E4DCE569DB2474CA8F145530C985448DCE960@exchange1.ad.uams.edu>
References: <4E4DCE569DB2474CA8F145530C985448DCE960@exchange1.ad.uams.edu>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.0.20021101164642.014a2eb8@stat.psu.edu>

While we are on this topic, it should be noted that the 2 microarrays per
subject should probably be done with a dye-swap.  This is because
dye-bias can be an important effect on the arrays.

Two good papers to look at are:

 M. Kathleen Kerr and Gary A. Churchill 

     Experimental design for gene expression
microarrays

     Biostatistics 2001 2: 183-201.

Design of studies using DNA microarrays

Richard Simon *, Michael D. Radmacher, Kevin Dobbin

Genetic Epidemiology

I am just getting into the area of design for micro-array studies,
but I think these 2 papers are essential reading.  

One point - with 15 micro-arrays and 2 per mouse, you can only have 7
mice if you use the same control for each array.  However, 2 mice
are hybridized to each array - hence there are designs which make more
efficient use of the micro-arrays, but there is a trade-off in
comparability. 

You could use as many as 15 mice in a "loop design" as
described by Kerr and Churchill.

--Naomi

