> [16,]  7.3557459    4    2
>
> Now I would like to add this matrix according to the indices j[,2] and
> j[,3]. In another word, I want to add j[1,] with j[5,], j[2,] with j[,6]
> and similarly j[,9] with j[,13] and so forth.
>
>
>
> The solution I have is as follows:
>
>
>
> j<-matrix(j[,1],ncol=1)
>
> jj<-lapply(fun.split.matrix(j,rep(1:2,each=8)),function(x)
> fun.split.matrix(x,rep(1:2,each=8))
>
> lapply(1:2,function(i) lapply(jj,colSums))
>
>
>
> Now, is there an even shorter way to achieve the same effect? If I have
> three indices or four indices the above method seems rather clumsy. I
> would welcome any suggestions that will achieve the same purpose in the
> shortest time and use the least memory as I have a huge list. I have tried
> using the multiplication by matrix approach but it seems to take a long time&

The tapply (table-apply) function should do what you want:

        tapply(j[,1], list(j[,2], j[,3]), sum)

