From jfox at mcmaster.ca  Mon Nov  4 22:07:47 2002
From: jfox at mcmaster.ca (John Fox)
Date: Mon, 04 Nov 2002 17:07:47 -0500
Subject: Re: 1) Row identification in if statement, and 2) creating
In-Reply-To: <076B2DE36164094C807D2BB199F9DBA8072D56@www.fisheries.ubc.ca>
References: <076B2DE36164094C807D2BB199F9DBA8072D56@www.fisheries.ubc.ca>
Message-ID: <5.0.2.1.0.20021104165613.00ac15e0@mcmail.cis.mcmaster.ca>

Dear Cindy,

I haven't tried to figure out what you're doing in detail, but maybe this 
will help:

At 01:49 PM 11/4/2002 -0800, Cindy Rejwan wrote:

> In the program, I am attempting to do one of three calculations on a
> dataset, depending on whether one of the variables (columns) in the dataset,
> called "TruncA" is equal to 1, 2, or 3.  The problem is that I do not know
> how to tell Splus that it needs to check on the identity of TruncA in each
> separate row.  Could you tell me what the correct commands are for this?

You can use cascading calls to ifelse; if the entries of TruncA can only be 
1, 2, or 3, then something like the following should work:

        ifelse(TruncA == 1, whatever happens for ones,
          ifelse(TruncA ==2, whatever happens for twos,
            whatever happens for threes))

The three "whatever happens" expressions must produce vectors of the same 
length as TruncA. See ?ifelse for details.

> Secondly, I am running a loop in the program, and after each run through the
> loop, I would like to create a new column of data.  At this stage, the
> command lines which I am using (listed below) simply create a single column
> (in the variable: "allystar1") for all combined runs through the loop rather
> than separate columns after each run.  Could you tell me how to correct this
> problem?

You could use cbind (column bind) in place of c (which you're using 
currently) to construct the result, but this is inefficient; it's generally 
a better idea to construct an empty data structure, such as a matrix (if 
that is appropriate), to hold the result; you can then index and replace 
the columns of this matrix. The general idea is as follows:

        result <- matrix(0, rows, columns)
        for (j in 1:columns) {
          result[,j] <- whatever produces the jth column
          }

Finally, you might find that you can avoid the loop by using a function in 
the apply family.

I hope that this helps,
 John
