From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Tue Nov  5 16:59:06 2002
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Tue, 05 Nov 2002 11:59:06 -0500
Subject: Re: using scan() in an expression
Message-ID: <200211051659.LAA15176@nightingale.hcp.med.harvard.edu>

I don't think you can.  When you type (or cut/paste) to the command line,
you are mixing two different streams of input:  input to the S parser,
and input to the scan command (which looks entirely different since it is
not an expression).  The location of the scan calls, and the blank lines
that terminate input to scan, implicitly switch back and forth between
the two streams at the appropriate places.  This would also work if you
run in batch mode (in Unix, anyway, haven't tried it on a PC), since all
that does is direct a file into standard input.  However, when you
enclose the whole mess in {} or equivalently read this with source(),
this is now a single express and the whole thing must be read by the parser.
Your lines of numbers are not valid expressions.  

You could turn the input into a valid literal expression using c(), read
from a separate file, or use input redirection as with batch mode.

