First, some background:

My project involves taking a large number of randomly-sized random samples
using the pair of functions appended to this message.

As can be seen, RS2.sample() produces a sample as the concatenation of a
given number of smaller samples, while RS2.meta.sample() uses RS2.sample()
to produce a desired number of samples and save them in an
appropriately-named directory.

The reason for this two-stage procedure is that despite the very large size
of the data sets to be worked with (108k observations, of which 20k to 60k
will be NAs), their extreme skewness can produce elementary samples whose
size can be counted on the fingers of one hand.

Thus although the eventual files for each sample are small, the whole
procedure involves working with a series of much larger objects.

A final point is that S-Plus is installed on the C: drive, which is also
where the input data resides.

Second, the problem:

Sometimes RS2.meta.sample() works (it takes about 20 minutes with my data),
but sometimes it doesn't.

When it works the hard disk activity light flashes at intervals, and for a
total number of times, consistent with the input data being read for each
round of sampling.

When it fails the disk activity light is lit virtually constantly, and the
onset of this condition is usually pretty rapid when it occurs. 

I take it that this is indicative of reading/writing to virtual memory
swamping other activity.

Since the procedure is capable of working without trouble I take it that
whatever infelicities there may be in the functions they are not necessarily
fatal.

Discussion of someone else's problems on the R list suggests that one issue
I should consider is Windows' memory management idiosyncrasies (I am using
S+ 2000 on Windows 95), although this is something which is well beyond my
expertise.

What do list members suggest as a way forward?

Julian Wells
OU Business School
The Open University
Walton Hall
Milton Keynes
MK7 6AA
United Kingdom
+44 1908 654658

Appendix
=======

RS2.sample <- function(p, k, sic.1, sic.2, how.many=100){
        test.frame <- na.omit(data.frame(cbind(p, k, sic.1, sic.2))) 
        how.big <- length(test.frame$p)
        coins <- test.frame$k/max(test.frame$k, na.rm=T)
        samples <- array(dim = c(how.big, how.many, 3))
        for (i in 1:how.many){
                tosses <- runif(how.big)
                samples[,i,1] <- ifelse(tosses>coins, NA, test.frame$p)
                samples[,i,2] <- ifelse(tosses>coins, NA, test.frame$sic.1)
                samples[,i,3] <- ifelse(tosses>coins, NA, test.frame$sic.2)
        }
        data <- as.vector(samples[,,1][!is.na(samples[,,1])])
        index.1 <- as.vector(samples[,,2][!is.na(samples[,,2])])
        index.2 <- as.vector(samples[,,2][!is.na(samples[,,3])])
        stuff <- cbind(data, index.1, index.2)
}

RS2.meta.sample <- function(p, k, sic.1, sic.2, folder.name="D:\\Data
sets\\F A M E\\Results\\", n=100){
        mkdir(paste(folder.name, deparse(substitute(p)), sep=""))
        for (i in 1:n) {
                assign(paste("sample", i, sep=""), RS2.sample(p, k, sic.1,
sic.2, how.many=100), where=1)
                data.dump(paste("sample", i, sep=""), paste(folder.name,
deparse(substitute(p)), "\\sample", i, sep=""))
                rm(paste("sample", i, sep=""))
                        }
}

