----------------------------------------------------------
ORIGINAL POST:

Splusers:

I am interested in fitting a series of linear models [ lm( ) ] to a
data.frame.
In SAS, one can use the simple commands

proc glm data = blah;
        by <stratifying variable>;
        ...model statement(s)....;
run;

to generate a set of results for every level of the stratifying variable.
How
do I do the same in Splus using lm( ) or lme( )?  I looked through the
"Guide to
Statistics, Vol. 1" and tried to search the archives and didn't find
anything (but 
that doesn't mean it wasn't there!).

-----------------------------------------------------------
REPLY #1:

What I do is use split to make a list of the data.
Then I use lapply to apply the same version of my function to the list.

I admit I have not tried this with anything as complex as lme, but it 
should work.

-----------------------------------------------------------
REPLY #2

Have you looked at lmlist()?

-----------------------------------------------------------
REPLY #3

Dear David,

You can use the by function; see the help page for an example with a linear 
model.

-----------------------------------------------------------
REPLY #4

Depending on what you need, you might use lmList (nlme library) or use
lm in conjunction with tapply or friends, e.g. (example built with R):

> library(nlme)
Loading required package: nls 
Loading required package: lattice 
> myData <- data.frame(resp = rnorm(100),
+   covariate = runif(100),
+   stratvar = sample(c("A", "B"), size = 100, replace = TRUE))
> 
> fm1 <- lmList(resp ~ covariate | stratvar, data = myData)
> fm1
Call:
  Model: resp ~ covariate | stratvar 
   Data: myData 

Coefficients:
  (Intercept)  covariate
A  0.14560010 -0.2495417
B  0.05003752 -0.1872397

Degrees of freedom: 100 total; 96 residual
Residual standard error: 0.8919162
> 
> tapply(seq(nrow(myData)),
+        myData$stratvar,
+        function(x) lm(resp ~ covariate, data = myData, subset = x))
$A

Call:
lm(formula = resp ~ covariate, data = myData, subset = x)

Coefficients:
(Intercept)    covariate  
     0.1456      -0.2495  

$B

Call:
lm(formula = resp ~ covariate, data = myData, subset = x)

Coefficients:
(Intercept)    covariate  
