Dear Eric,

At 11:45 AM 11/10/2002 -0500, Eric Turkheimer wrote:

> This is more of a methods question than an S question per se, but I
> thought it might find a better answer here than on the usenet stat
> groups.  When computing a lowess smooth, you first select a span in which
> to compute the weights, then compute a set of weights within the span.  My
> question is, given that weights are computed in terms of distance of the
> weighted point from the point where the smooth is being computed, why
> bother with the span at all?  Why not just weight the entire set of
> observations in terms of a tricube function of distance?

The span determines the distance at which the tricube weights fall to zero 
and therefore determines how local the regression is; nothing prevents you 
from using a span of 1.0 or even larger (loess permit this,  lowess does 
not), but doing so will produce a smoother regression than using a smaller 
span. In other words, the span allows you to adjust the smoothness of the 
result.

I hope that this helps,
 John
