From jfox at mcmaster.ca  Mon Nov 11 00:34:59 2002
From: jfox at mcmaster.ca (John Fox)
Date: Sun, 10 Nov 2002 19:34:59 -0500
Subject: Re: residuals large when predicted value small, small when
In-Reply-To: <sdce7e85.089@medicine.umaryland.edu>
References: <sdce7e85.089@medicine.umaryland.edu>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20021110193115.01dd4408@mcmail.cis.mcmaster.ca>

Dear John,

Transformations down the "ladder" of powers and roots -- e.g., square-root, 
log, inverse -- tend to correct a tendency of spread to increase with 
level; transformations up the ladder -- e.g., square, cube -- can help when 
spread decreases with level. All this presupposes that the data values are 
positive.

There are good reasons, however, why spread often increases with level. It 
would probably help to try to understand why you have less variation when 
average value of the response is large.

I hope that this help,
 John

