From paulda at BATTELLE.ORG  Mon Nov 11 21:50:38 2002
From: paulda at BATTELLE.ORG (Paul, David A)
Date: Mon, 11 Nov 2002 16:50:38 -0500
Subject: SUMMARY: lm( ) and stratification
Message-ID: <CFA6739DD532764AA51E7459970BF0E205D0A91E@ns-bco-mse3.im.battelle.org>

By adding the option "singular.ok = T" to my original code, I
was able to reproduced exactly what PROC MIXED yielded when 
using the CLASS X1 statement.  Thanks much to David Smith
of Insightful for pointing this out to me!  I have attached 
his entire reply below.

-David Paul

-----------------------------------------------------
My guess is that for some subsets of your data 
(as defined by foo$VAR1) not all the potential levels 
of the factor VAR3 are represented in your subset.  
S-PLUS is throwing an error to inform you that it can't 
estimate a coefficient for the dummy variable representing 
that level.

When you subset a factor in S-PLUS, it remembers all the 
possible levels, even if the subset doesn't contain 
representatives of all of them.  The as.factor() call 
essentially recreates the factor remembering only the 
levels that appear, which is why the error doesn't occur 
in that case.  Another alternative is to include the 
singular.ok option to lm(), which is probably a better choice 
if you want to assemble the coefficients at the end; 
otherwise the coefficient vectors will be of different 
lengths.

# David Smith

