From naomi at stat.psu.edu  Wed Nov 13 14:05:45 2002
From: naomi at stat.psu.edu (Naomi Altman)
Date: Wed, 13 Nov 2002 09:05:45 -0500
Subject: Re: heterogenous variance of random effect in lme
In-Reply-To: <5.1.0.14.2.20021112193838.00ad4540@uio-pop.uio.no>
References: <5.1.0.14.2.20021112193838.00ad4540@uio-pop.uio.no>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.0.20021113085834.014ba878@stat.psu.edu>

The random statement allows you to enter a model.  Then the variance
depends on the level of the model.  Alternatively, you can used
pdBlocked to set up a block diagonal random effects variance
matrix.  The variances are estimated by ML or REML.  

The book by Pinheiro and Bates is excellent.  Unfortunately, you do
have to read much of the book to understand what is going on, but it is
well-written.  Some abstracts of it appear to be available in the
on-line documentation.

I have had problems with convergence using Splus 2000, but these went
away with Splus 6.0 and higher.  

e.g.
random=pdBlocked(list(pdIdent(~
as.matrix(z1) -1),pdIdent(~as.matrix(z2)-1 )

Good luck,

Naomi Altman

